=== meta
title = "Reuse without copying"
chapter = "6 / 7"
===

# {{title}} {#u-top}

Copying is how documents drift. GEML lets one document *point* at content in
another and render it in place — nothing is written here, and editing the source
moves every consumer.

## A phrase, projected inline {#u-inline}

`![[#id]]` pulls a single-paragraph `text` block into a sentence. System status
right now: ![[all_types_demo.geml#status_phrase]] — that clause lives in
`all_types_demo.geml`, not here.

Only a `text` block holding **one paragraph** may be projected that way — the
parser enforces it, because a phrase has nowhere to put a heading or a table.

A **coordinate** may be projected too, and it crosses documents like anything
else. Chapter 3's company total is ![[ch03-coordinates.geml#c-fy[summary]["FY"]]],
read out of the reported row of a `view` in another file — a number this sentence
does not contain and cannot get wrong.

## A whole block, embedded {#u-block}

=== embed {#u-arch src=all_types_demo.geml#arch_flow}
===

That is a Mermaid diagram kept in another file — design assets stay separate
from the report that uses them.

## A chain, resolved across files {#u-chain}

=== embed {#u-chart src=example_transclusion.geml#q3_chart}
===

That embed pulls a chart, and the chart itself binds to a table in *its* own
file. Two hops, both checked when the document is parsed. A cycle would be an
error naming every document in it, and depth is bounded — a chain cannot recurse
the renderer to death.

## A section from this very document {#u-self}

=== embed {#u-mirror src=#u-inline}
===

`src=#id` on a heading pulls the whole section. The block above mirrors
[[#u-inline]] — including the projections inside it, which resolve again.

## Only the heading {#u-part}

=== embed {src=ch02-relation.geml#r-group part=head}
===

`part=head` takes the head line and leaves the body — useful for building an
index out of other documents' headings without dragging their content along.

=== note {.tip #u-why}
None of this is preprocessing. The references live in the model, so `geml check`
fails the build when one breaks, and an agent editing the source knows exactly
which documents it just changed.
===
